  var POSTS={
    alignment:{title:'Why a straight steering wheel saves tyres',cat:'Alignment',img:'assets/img/blog-alignment.webp',url:'blog-alignment.html',read:'6 min read',excerpt:'Uneven toe can scrub a healthy tyre surprisingly quickly. Learn what an alignment report tells you and when geometry should be checked.',copy:['Modern four-wheel geometry compares your vehicle against manufacturer targets. Small toe errors can scrub rubber every mile and make a new set of tyres age unevenly.','A pothole strike, suspension work, fresh tyres, steering pull or an off-centre steering wheel are sensible reasons to check geometry instead of waiting for visible wear.','Ask for the before-and-after values and keep the report. It gives future wear a useful reference point and confirms exactly what was adjusted.']},
    pressure:{title:'The 5-minute pressure habit before a motorway run',cat:'Tyre Care',img:'assets/img/blog-pressure.webp',url:'blog-pressure.html',read:'5 min read',excerpt:'A cold-pressure check is one of the fastest ways to protect grip, tyre life and fuel efficiency before a long journey.',copy:['Check pressures cold before the car has travelled far. Heat can temporarily raise pressure and hide an under-inflated starting point.','Use the pressure placard supplied by the vehicle manufacturer rather than the maximum number moulded into the tyre sidewall. Adjust for load where the manufacturer specifies it.','Look for cuts, bulges, nails and uneven shoulders while you measure. Pressure is useful, but a visual inspection often catches the problem first.']},
    vibration:{title:'Wheel vibration: balance problem or something else?',cat:'Workshop',img:'assets/img/apextread-wheel-alignment.webp',url:'blog-vibration.html',read:'7 min read',excerpt:'Speed-related vibration can come from balance, tyre uniformity, wheel damage or suspension. Here is how a workshop narrows it down.',copy:['A vibration that appears in a narrow speed band often points to a wheel or tyre issue, but balance is only one possible cause.','Bent wheels, tyre uniformity, hub runout and worn suspension parts can imitate imbalance. The location of the vibration and when it appears help guide the first checks.','Road-force testing and runout measurement help identify assemblies that balance numerically but still create vibration on the road.']},
    puncture:{title:'Repair or replace? Reading a puncture correctly',cat:'Tyre Care',img:'assets/img/apextread-puncture-repair.webp',url:'blog-puncture.html',read:'6 min read',excerpt:'Not every puncture should be repaired. Position, casing condition and previous damage decide whether the tyre can safely return to service.',copy:['A professional repair is generally limited to suitable damage within the tread area, away from the shoulder and sidewall. The tyre also needs enough remaining tread and a healthy casing.','Removing the tyre for an internal inspection catches heat damage, inner-liner injury and hidden casing problems that an external plug-only check can miss.','Repeated punctures in the same area, visible sidewall damage or a casing that has been driven flat are reasons to plan replacement rather than force a repair.']},
    nitrogen:{title:'Does nitrogen actually help your tyres?',cat:'Tyre Care',img:'assets/img/showroom.webp',url:'blog-nitrogen.html',read:'4 min read',excerpt:'Nitrogen can slow pressure drift, but it is not a cure for leaks. Understand where it helps and where regular air is perfectly adequate.',copy:['Nitrogen can reduce moisture and may allow pressure to drift more slowly over time, which is useful where vehicles follow disciplined service intervals.','It will not fix an existing leak or a slow puncture, and topping a nitrogen-filled tyre with normal compressed air is preferable to driving under-inflated.','For most drivers, checking pressure regularly matters far more than the gas used. Fleets and high-mileage vehicles may value the slower drift as part of planned maintenance.']},
    inspection:{title:'A practical monthly tyre inspection anyone can do',cat:'Tyre Care',img:'assets/img/blog-inspection.webp',url:'blog-inspection.html',read:'8 min read',excerpt:'Use pressure, tread, tyre age and sidewall condition to spot problems before the car starts pulling, vibrating or warning you.',copy:['Start with cold pressures and compare all four tyres. A repeated difference on one corner can point to a slow leak, valve problem or damage that deserves inspection.','Check tread across the inner, centre and outer sections. One-sided wear can indicate geometry issues, while both shoulders may suggest chronic under-inflation.','Inspect sidewalls for cuts, bulges and impact marks, then check the tyre manufacturing date when age is a concern. If anything looks unusual, get the tyre inspected before a long journey.']},
    wetgrip:{title:'Wet grip: when tread depth starts to matter more',cat:'Tyre Care',img:'assets/img/tyre-safety-check.webp',url:'blog-wetgrip.html',read:'6 min read',excerpt:'Legal tread depth is only one reference point. Heavy rain, standing water and ageing rubber can change the margin you feel on the road.',copy:['Tread channels move water away from the contact patch. As depth reduces, the tyre has less capacity to clear standing water, especially at higher speeds.','Tyre compound, pressure, vehicle load and road surface all influence wet grip. That is why two tyres with similar measured tread can feel different in poor weather.','Monitor tread across the full width and plan replacement around your real driving conditions, not only the final legal threshold.']},
    tpmsguide:{title:'TPMS warning light on? What to check before replacing a sensor',cat:'Workshop',img:'assets/img/apextread-tpms-pressure.webp',url:'blog-tpms-guide.html',read:'5 min read',excerpt:'A pressure warning does not automatically mean a failed sensor. Start with actual tyre pressure and then diagnose the system.',copy:['First confirm the pressure in every tyre with an accurate gauge. Temperature changes and a genuine slow leak are more common than electronic failure.','If pressures are correct but the warning remains, the system may need a reset, relearn or sensor diagnosis depending on the vehicle.','A workshop can identify which sensor is reporting incorrectly and check battery or communication status before parts are replaced unnecessarily.']},
    rotationguide:{title:'Tyre rotation: when it helps and when it does not',cat:'Workshop',img:'assets/img/apextread-tyre-rotation.webp',url:'blog-rotation-guide.html',read:'5 min read',excerpt:'Rotation can even out wear, but staggered sizes, directional tyres and existing wear patterns change what is appropriate for your vehicle.',copy:['Rotation aims to share wear between positions that work the tyres differently. Front-driven cars often load steering and drive duties onto the front axle.','The correct pattern depends on tyre direction, front/rear sizes and vehicle manufacturer guidance. Some performance setups cannot be rotated front to rear at all.','If a tyre already shows strong one-sided or cupped wear, investigate alignment, balance or suspension first rather than treating rotation as the fix.']}
  };
  function $(s,p){return (p||document).querySelector(s)} function $$(s,p){return Array.from((p||document).querySelectorAll(s))}
  var memoryStore={};
  function storageGet(kind,k){try{var store=kind==='session'?window.sessionStorage:window.localStorage;return store.getItem(k)}catch(e){return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(memoryStore,k)?memoryStore[k]:null}}
  function storageSet(kind,k,v){try{var store=kind==='session'?window.sessionStorage:window.localStorage;store.setItem(k,v)}catch(e){memoryStore[k]=String(v)}}
  function storageRemove(kind,k){try{var store=kind==='session'?window.sessionStorage:window.localStorage;store.removeItem(k)}catch(e){delete memoryStore[k]}}
  function read(k,f){try{var v=JSON.parse(storageGet('local',k));return v==null?f:v}catch(e){try{var mv=JSON.parse(memoryStore[k]);return mv==null?f:mv}catch(_){return f}}}
  function write(k,v){storageSet('local',k,JSON.stringify(v))}
  function esc(v){return String(v==null?'':v).replace(/[&<>"']/g,function(m){return {'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[m]})}
